  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/1/1c/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_97.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Thor saves a military pilot who's blacked-out due to lack of oxygen.&nbsp; As he returns to his office, he decides momentarily to tell Jane Foster how he feels about her, and she too can sense it, even going so far as to tell him to say it.&nbsp; Blake even calls her \"my darling,\" but there's one thing he must do first, to which she replies, \"There is <i>always</i> just one more thing!\"&nbsp; Turns out that one thing is to ask Odin if he can marry a mortal, to which Odin replies \"Have you taken leave of your <i>senses</i>?\" and flatly refuses his petition.&nbsp; From Asgard, unable to leave, Loki watches all, wanting to strike, but he can't leave, so he looks for a \"catspaw, an agent to do the task for me!\"\n\nMeanwhile, the newspapers are talking about a \"Menace From Volcano Identified as Lava Man!\"&nbsp; Loki decides this will be the perfect agent for him to defeat Thor.&nbsp; But Thor has got himself tied up in knots over Jane, who comes to him to announce she's quitting to work for another doctor - she can't take the uncertainty anymore.&nbsp; Blake says \"Then - this is goodbye, Jane.\"\n\nAs he walks alone on the street, dejected, the Lava Man strikes, claiming the dry surface of the Earth for the Lava People!&nbsp; As he and Thor engage in battle, Thor can hear Loki's sinister laughter from above.&nbsp; A panel or two later, Thor indicates that the city has been evacuated, even though Lava Man appeared for the first time a few panels earlier, in a street filled with people - <i>that <b>has</b> to be the absolute fastest evacuation in recorded history</i>!&nbsp; The struggle continues with Thor finally forcing the Lava Man into a volcano and back down to his own people.\n\nThor returns his human identity, to try and coax Jane to stay, but she is again upset that he didn't even try to find her while the city was threatened.&nbsp; She's done with him, and will now work for Dr. Bruce Andrews!&nbsp; \"A woman wants a <i>man</i>, Doctor Blake - not a timid mouse!\"\n\nBlake stares sadly out the window...",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Another weak outing from <i>Journey Into Mystery</i>, which for this issue might have been better named <i>Descent into Unrequited Romance</i>.&nbsp; Even though Blake/Thor knows Odin refuses to let him reveal himself to Jane, he asks anyway that he be allowed to marry her.&nbsp; Did he expect a different answer this time?&nbsp; <i>Really</i>?&nbsp; The whole thing smells of Stan Lee perhaps returning to write the monthly Thor issue after having <i>just penned</i> one of the romance comics of the day - <i>major</i> bleed-over -- <i>bleah</i>.\n\nJane finally as much as telling Blake her feelings - with the \"my darling\" slip in there too - followed by him still struggling to announce his feelings is too much for her.&nbsp; But the quitting seems to happen too fast - there is no hint of the possibility before this point.&nbsp; Way too forced if you ask me.&nbsp; And why is it required that a nurse - who is <i>paid</i> for the job - be required to work for a \"man\" rather than a \"mouse?\"&nbsp; Does Dr. Andrews simply pay better?&nbsp; I don't get it....\n\nAlso, the Lava Man and Loki <i>both</i> seemed crammed into the book solely so there will be a fight scene for Kirby to draw.&nbsp; (Not to mention the record speed of city-wide evacuation I commented on above.)&nbsp; None of this works for me at all!\n\nThe Lava Man is one of the \"Lava Men,\" who next appear in <i>Avengers</i> #5, coming up in just a few short months.\n\nThis issue was drawn by Kirby, but inked by Don Heck, who I will confess did a great job smoothing out some of Kirby's rough angles, particularly in the faces (e.g. top-right panel of page 3).&nbsp; It was an interesting pairing I don't believe I've ever seen before (Heck normally pencils).&nbsp; Some of the latter-half panels looked more like Heck's work than Kirby's.&nbsp; With Kirby having to do Thor, FF, the FF Annual, Avengers, and X-Men, it could be that he was too rushed to be able to provide full pencils and had to rely on another to tidy things up.&nbsp; It has that feel to it.\n\nCurious that on the splash page the letters of the text \"Lava Man\" are covered by what looks more like snow than lava.&nbsp; It's a weird, and perhaps unintentional effect.\n\n\n<b>Tales of Asgard </b>makes its first appearance in this issue as well, providing some background into the Norse legends, the good ones being the Aesir and the bad ones the Frost Giants, even mentioning the Ymir, the greatest of these Frost Giants, and Surtur (this one-panel marks his first appearance in fact).&nbsp; Buri was the first of the Aesir, who was the grandfather of Odin himself, greatest of all the Norse gods, who slayed the last of the Ice Giants.\n\nOdin and his two brothers protected Earth with a ring around the planet, blanked by the branches of Yggdrasil, the magic tree, which protected it \"while awaiting the coming of <i>man</i>!\"\n\nLee and Kirby presumably open up the Norse mythology texts and share some of its material, which we can only assume will be woven into the fabric of the Marvel/Norse gods.&nbsp; We'll see....&nbsp; We're promised more to come in the next issue!\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME - write something</b>",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}